9	 *
10	 * `test/customer-actions-guards.test.ts` proves that every export of the
11	 * CUSTOMER-facing action modules identifies its caller — but its file list stops
12	 * at the customer surface, so the admin-only modules (refunds, staff roles, node
13	 * lifecycle, abuse, gift codes, mass mail…) had no equivalent net. Every export
14	 * of a `"use server"` file is a public network endpoint reachable by curl, so an
15	 * admin action whose `requireAdmin()`/`requireArea()` was dropped in a refactor
16	 * would be a privilege hole with nothing to catch it. This is that net.
17	 *
18	 * A guard here is not merely "authenticates" — it must be the RIGHT authority.
19	 * The area/role each module enforces is asserted separately (see EXPECTED below),
20	 * so a module cannot pass by swapping `requireAdmin` for a bare `requireUser`.
21	 *
22	 * Source is comment-stripped first: this repo documents its incidents in the code
23	 * and a raw grep matches the note, not the call. See test/_source-tools.ts.
